RITE PARENT BODIES - IOPscience (https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0004-637X/809/1/6)\n  45. NATURE'S STARSHIPS. I. OBSERVED ABUNDANCES AND RELATIVE FREQUENCIES OF AMINO ACIDS IN METEORITES - IOPscience (https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0004-637X/783/2/140)\n  46. Hypothesis Article A Thermodynamic Basis for Prebiotic Amino Acid Synthesis (https://www.mso.anu.edu.au/~aditya/psi_seminars/2011/Pudritz/Higgs_and_Pudritz_2009.pdf)\n  47. Spectroscopy of Hydrothermal Reactions 25: Kinetics of the Decarboxylation of Protein Amino Acids and the Effect of Side Chains on Hydrothermal Stability (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/231633952_Spectroscopy_of_Hydrothermal_Reactions_25_Kinetics_of_the_Decarboxylation_of_Protein_Amino_Acids_and_the_Effect_of_Side_Chains_on_Hydrothermal_Stability)\n  48. The reaction kinetics of alanine and glycine under hydrothermal conditions (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/223355391_The_reaction_kinetics_of_alanine_and_glycine_under_hydrothermal_conditions)\n  49. Hydrothermal Reactions of Biomolecules Relevant for Microalgae Liquefaction (https://pubs.acs.org/iecred/article/54/47/11733/949086/Hydrothermal-Reactions-of-Biomolecules-Relevant)\n  50. Thermal Stability of Threonine in the Presence of a Marine Polyphenolic Material (https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.151.3707.214)\n  51. Radiolytic Decomposition and Radiation Sensitivity of Amino Acids in Aqueous Solution by Gamma Irradiation\\* (https://academic.oup.com/jrr/article/6/3-4/130/984246)\n  52. T- and pH-dependent OH radical reaction kinetics with glycine, alanine, serine, and threonine in the aqueous phase - Physical Chemistry Chemical Physics (RSC Publishing) (https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlelanding/2022/cp/d1cp05186e)\n  53. Primordial synthesis of amines and amino acids in a 1958 Miller H2S-rich spark discharge experiment (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.1019191108)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Threonine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"We construct a comprehensive prebiotic synthesis network for threonine, tracing ten plausible pathways rooted in experimentally supported and literature-mandated geochemical scenarios. The dominant subnetworks are: (1) Strecker-type synthesis from C3 hydroxyaldehyde (lactaldehyde) derived via homologation and photoreduction of HCN/aldehyde substrates; (2) metal-chelate-catalyzed aldol-type condensations of glycine and acetaldehyde, producing \u03b2-hydroxy-\u03b1-amino acids; (3) 'shotgun' radical chemistry in atmospheric/surface discharge contexts; and (4) rare sugar-side product transformations via coupling and reduction. Intermediates are sourced by UV, photoreductive, mineral-catalyzed, or atmospheric processes. Hydrothermal routes are not supported, and biological analogues (homoserine pathway) remain hypothetical at best. The network reflects all documented limitations: low yields, lack of stereoselectivity, instability of threonine, and the central challenge posed by the availability of C2/C3 carbonyl intermediates. Each connection is justified by the cited literature or marked as speculative with chemical reasoning.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"HOCH2CHO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyacetaldehyde\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h2,4H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH3CHO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"HOCH2CH(OH)CH=O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,3-dihydroxypropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyceraldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c4-1-2(5)3-6/h2,4-6H,1,3H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH3CH(OH)CH=O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6O2/c1-3(5)2-4/h2-3,5H,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH3CH(OH)CH(CN)NH2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"threonine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H8N2O/c1-3(7)2-4(5)6/h2-3,7H,1H3,(H2,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"threonine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H9NO3/c1-2(6)3(7)4(5)8/h2-3,6-7H,1,5H2\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": null,\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4N2/c3-1-2-4/h1,3H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H5NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-aminopropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": null,\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO/c4-2-1-3-5/h3,5H,1-2,4H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2,4H,1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanediol\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ethylene glycol\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H6O2/c1-2-3/h3H,1-2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-2-hydroxypropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"serine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO3/c1-2(6)3(7)4/h2,6-7H,1,4H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid (allo)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"allothreonine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H9NO3/c1-2(6)3(7)4(5)8/h2-3,6-7H,1,5H2/t2-,3+\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Formose reaction: HCN and formaldehyde to glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite clay\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite clay\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"neutral to mildly alkaline; wet-dry cycles\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Oligomerization of formaldehyde, catalyzed by minerals and cyanide, yields C2 sugars including glycolaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Formose chemistry yields glycolaldehyde in plausible surface settings (Weber & Miller, 1981; Patel et al., Nat. Chem. 2015). Montmorillonite and cyanide both promote this process.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Reduction of glycolaldehyde to ethylene glycol\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"H2S\", \"FeS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS, H2S\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"reducing, UV exposure possible\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reductive conversion of glycolaldehyde to ethylene glycol on iron-sulfide in presence of H2S.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Reduction demonstrated in cyanosulfidic sequences (Ritson & Sutherland, Angew. Chem. 2013).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxidation of ethylene glycol to glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"UV light\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV photolysis\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photochemical oxidation provides recycling of glycolaldehyde from ethylene glycol.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Surface UV allows bidirectional conversion between C2 alcohols and aldehydes, as observed in ice photolysis (Nuevo et al., 2008).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Homologation: Glycolaldehyde to glyceraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"borate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"borate minerals\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"wet-dry cycles favor selectivity\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldol addition of formaldehyde to glycolaldehyde forms glyceraldehyde; borate stabilizes sugar against decomposition.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Borate promotes C3 sugar formation and selectivity (Ritson & Sutherland, 2013).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Reduction of glyceraldehyde to lactaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I) (as CuCN)\", \"H2S\", \"UV light\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I), H2S, UV\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"mildly acidic to neutral; photoredox\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photoreduction of C3 sugar to hydroxypropanal via cyanosulfidic chemistry.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Photoreduction demonstrated by Ritson & Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015); lactaldehyde forms as Strecker precursor.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker reaction: lactaldehyde to threonine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"neutral pH, wet\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Imine formation between lactaldehyde and ammonia, followed by cyanide addition, gives 2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Strecker pathway for threonine established; aminonitrile formation confirmed but not isolated yield (Ritson & Sutherland, 2013; Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of threonine aminonitrile to threonine and allothreonine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite clay\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"weakly acidic or hydrothermal, slow step\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential hydrolysis of nitrile to amide and then carboxylic acid, yielding both threonine and allothreonine due to epimerization.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported by classic Strecker/abiotic hydrolysis paths; lack of selectivity noted (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Formose-access: glycolaldehyde and ammonia to glycine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"mildly basic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Strecker condensation on glycolaldehyde; further hydrolysis yields glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Standard Strecker; routine in meteorite models and experiments (Cobb & Pudritz 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of glycine aminonitrile to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite clay\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite clay\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"slow step\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile hydrolyzed to acid; hydrolysis assisted by clay/mineral and warm conditions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Routine in prebiotic models, also observed in parent body weathering (Cobb & Pudritz 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldol-type condensation: glycine + acetaldehyde to threonine and allothreonine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(II) ions\", \"basic pH\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(II) chelate\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"moderate base, wet-dry cycling\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cu(II)-glycinate complex enables nucleophilic addition to acetaldehyde; \u03b2-hydroxy amino acid forms; threo/allo ratio base-dependent.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Documented experimentally (Sato et al., 1957; Weber & Miller 1981); high yield but metal/base loading not fully prebiotic.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetaldehyde formation by glycolaldehyde reduction\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"H2S\", \"FeS\", \"UV\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS, H2S, UV\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"reducing, photoreductive\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Glycolaldehyde undergoes reductive deoxygenation to acetaldehyde under cyanosulfidic and photochemical conditions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Photoreduction confirmed in Sutherland\u2019s cyanosulfidic network (Ritson & Sutherland, 2013; Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycine + formaldehyde to serine by Strecker\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite clay\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"neutral to mildly basic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Glycine condenses via Strecker with formaldehyde and ammonia to yield serine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Observed as a major product in spark and ice-photolysis experiments (Ring et al., 1972; Nuevo et al., 2008).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Serine methylation to threonine (speculative)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite\", \"UV light\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"requires simultaneous methyl source, little direct evidence\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Serine undergoes C-methylation at \u03b2-carbon by acetaldehyde, yielding threonine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative; included as a plausible primitive methylation but unsupported in prebiotic setting. Provides network overlap.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Random radical/rainbow chemistry (shotgun) yields trace threonine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"spark discharge\", \"H2S\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"lightning/spark, H2S-rich, trace yields\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sulfur and radical chemistry on basic starting feedstock yields a complex mix of amino acids including threonine and allothreonine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Experimentally confirmed in H2S-rich Miller apparatus (Ring et al., 1972; Parker et al., 2011); only trace product, low selectivity.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker from lactaldehyde\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN and formaldehyde build successively to glycolaldehyde, glyceraldehyde, then photoreduction to lactaldehyde. Lactaldehyde undergoes Strecker aminonitrile formation and hydrolysis, giving threonine and allothreonine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldol condensation of glycine and acetaldehyde (copper-catalyzed)\",\n      \"description\": \"Glycolaldehyde + ammonia + HCN gives glycine; glycolaldehyde is also photoreduced to acetaldehyde; glycine and acetaldehyde condense via Cu(II) catalysis to form threonine and allothreonine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Shotgun discharge chemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"Direct spark/radical chemistry on simple feedstocks in H2S-rich reducing atmosphere yields trace threonine (and allothreonine) with no selectivity.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Photoreductive pathway with ethylene glycol loop\",\n      \"description\": \"Glycolaldehyde is reduced to ethylene glycol, which is re-oxidized to glycolaldehyde, feeding into the Strecker or aldol routes; cycle allows regeneration under alternating redox surface conditions.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker on sequential triose/hydroxypropanal\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyceraldehyde, generated via formose-type chemistry, is photoreduced in the presence of HCN and ammonia to give directly lactaldehyde or other C3 hydroxy-aldehydes, which then undergo Strecker synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Serine methylation route (speculative)\",\n      \"description\": \"Glycine and formaldehyde yield serine; serine is then C-methylated at the \u03b2 carbon by acetaldehyde on clay or under UV, giving threonine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_013\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Parallel Strecker/aldol split hub from glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"description\": \"Network diversification from glycolaldehyde: one branch goes via aminonitrile to glycine, another to acetaldehyde, opening both Strecker and aldol routes to threonine, increasing possible product complexity.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Serine-centric pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Serine, generated via Strecker, is utilized as a hub for conversion to threonine (speculative or via trace radical chemistry), providing an alternative link between two networks.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Lactic acid side cycle (serine to lactic acid to lactaldehyde)\",\n      \"description\": \"Serine undergoes deamination or dehydration to lactic acid; lactic acid is reduced to lactaldehyde, which undergoes the Strecker sequence to threonine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_013\", \"