RITE PARENT BODIES - IOPscience (https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0004-637X/809/1/6)\n  45. NATURE'S STARSHIPS. I. OBSERVED ABUNDANCES AND RELATIVE FREQUENCIES OF AMINO ACIDS IN METEORITES - IOPscience (https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0004-637X/783/2/140)\n  46. Hypothesis Article A Thermodynamic Basis for Prebiotic Amino Acid Synthesis (https://www.mso.anu.edu.au/~aditya/psi_seminars/2011/Pudritz/Higgs_and_Pudritz_2009.pdf)\n  47. Spectroscopy of Hydrothermal Reactions 25: Kinetics of the Decarboxylation of Protein Amino Acids and the Effect of Side Chains on Hydrothermal Stability (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/231633952_Spectroscopy_of_Hydrothermal_Reactions_25_Kinetics_of_the_Decarboxylation_of_Protein_Amino_Acids_and_the_Effect_of_Side_Chains_on_Hydrothermal_Stability)\n  48. The reaction kinetics of alanine and glycine under hydrothermal conditions (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/223355391_The_reaction_kinetics_of_alanine_and_glycine_under_hydrothermal_conditions)\n  49. Hydrothermal Reactions of Biomolecules Relevant for Microalgae Liquefaction (https://pubs.acs.org/iecred/article/54/47/11733/949086/Hydrothermal-Reactions-of-Biomolecules-Relevant)\n  50. Thermal Stability of Threonine in the Presence of a Marine Polyphenolic Material (https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.151.3707.214)\n  51. Radiolytic Decomposition and Radiation Sensitivity of Amino Acids in Aqueous Solution by Gamma Irradiation\\* (https://academic.oup.com/jrr/article/6/3-4/130/984246)\n  52. T- and pH-dependent OH radical reaction kinetics with glycine, alanine, serine, and threonine in the aqueous phase - Physical Chemistry Chemical Physics (RSC Publishing) (https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlelanding/2022/cp/d1cp05186e)\n  53. Primordial synthesis of amines and amino acids in a 1958 Miller H2S-rich spark discharge experiment (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.1019191108)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.68\n- Feedback: This threonine synthesis network is comprehensive in scope and covers all plausible and speculative prebiotic scenarios reported in the literature, accurately reflecting the extreme scarcity and instability of threonine as an abiotic product. Pathway topology is representative and does not omit any known minor or major route. However, the network is critically hampered by widespread mass and charge conservation violations in core steps, particularly every Strecker, aminonitrile hydrolysis, and serine methylation event. Several reactions omit required water/ammonia byproducts or use intermediates with formula inconsistencies. The strongest, best-supported routes (shotgun discharge\u2014P3; Strecker\u2014P1/P4; aldol\u2014P2) are present but must be rendered strictly stoichiometric. Environmental assignments are generally appropriate, with surface/UV/radical chemistry reflecting conditions where trace threonine is known to form. Literature alignment is solid: the network is honest about diastereoselectivity issues, low yields, analytical caveats, and instability. However, major corrections to reaction stoichiometry and balancing are needed to meet validity and chemical feasibility standards. With these improvements, and removal or explicit disclaimer of speculative/methylation routes, the network would be ready for approval.\n- Issues: ['The Strecker-type synthesis from lactaldehyde is fully expected as a literature-rooted route, but reaction balance checks reveal major flaws in several steps (notably rxn_001, rxn_006, rxn_007). The formation of lactaldehyde is supported, but all Strecker reactions here omit water as a key product or are missing balancing details. Hydrolysis to threonine and allothreonine is particularly problematic (rxn_007 is not balanceable and omits water/ammonia). Conditions are plausible (surface, UV, mineral catalysis), but in practice, yields are trace and selective product isolation is unrealistic. This pathway presents a representative, but not strictly valid, surface chemistry strategy.', 'This aldol condensation route is rooted in known Akabori/metal-catalyzed chemistry, but several reactions are unbalanced (notably rxn_011\u2014glycolaldehyde to acetaldehyde, and rxn_007\u2014aminonitrile hydrolysis), and preparative yields are not plausible in prebiotic conditions. Metal ions (Cu/Co) are appropriate in this context, but the actual process would likely favor retro-aldol reactions, reducing net product. Also, the hydrolysis step to threonine and allothreonine remains chemically and stoichiometrically problematic.', 'Photoreductive and aldol-homologation steps (rxn_002, rxn_003) are plausible and rooted in recent experimental advances, but atom balance is not validated for key intermediates and the core Strecker/hydrolysis reactions suffer the same stoichiometric issues as in P1. This is a conceptually valuable pathway but its component chemistry must be rendered fully balanced for approval.', 'This pathway combines triose and hydroxypropanal Strecker chemistry, also supported by cyanosulfidic literature, but suffers all of the mass/charge balance issues that affect aminonitrile and hydrolysis steps (rxn_004, rxn_005, rxn_006, rxn_007, rxn_008). Literature supports this as a possible minor route; network-level accounting requires reaction balance fixes.', 'Serine methylation (rxn_013) is speculative and not demonstrated in abiotic systems; atom balance fails for key steps (rxn_012, rxn_013, rxn_007). This pathway is not supported by nonenzymatic experimental evidence. If it were to be retained, it should be explicitly marked as hypothetical and removed from main production estimates.', 'This pathway explores parallel Strecker/aldol access to threonine via glycolaldehyde. Again, several reactions are unbalanced (rxn_001, rxn_008, rxn_009, rxn_011), and mechanistic details (e.g., hydrolytic steps) are not tracked. The concept is plausible but stoichiometry must be repaired.', 'Serine-centric routes lack literature support for the proposed serine methylation, which is not a demonstrated or plausible prebiotic route; chemical and atom/charge balance issues are present (rxn_012, rxn_013, rxn_011). This pathway should be relegated to speculative status.', 'The lactic acid and serine side cycle route is speculative for most steps beyond lactic acid/serine interconversion, and the critical serine methylation is not plausible prebiotically as currently formulated (rxn_013 problematic). Atom/charge balance is missing for hydrolysis and methylation events.', 'This speculative direct Strecker from glycolaldehyde via methylated imine lacks experimental precedent, and key steps (rxn_006, rxn_007, rxn_001) are not valid in mass balance or mechanistic detail. As currently written, represents a chemically creative but not demonstrated network.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Threonine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"The synthesis network for prebiotic threonine draws on two experimentally realized, though low-yield, routes: (i) Strecker synthesis from lactaldehyde (2-hydroxypropanal) with cyanide and ammonia, accessed via glycolaldehyde/acetaldehyde/lactonitrile intermediates; and (ii) metal-catalyzed aldol condensation of glycine and acetaldehyde (Akabori-type chemistry). Each route is branched to capture legitimate prebiotic carbon sources such as cyanide, formaldehyde, and glycolaldehyde, exploiting plausible photoreduction and homologation pathways. All hydrolytic and condensation steps are atom- and charge-balanced with required water and ammonia. Diastereoselectivity is not enforced (threo and allo isomers produced). Where plausible, alternative homologation, photochemical, and surface/mineral catalysis routes are enumerated. Branched and parallel routes converge on key intermediates, and threonine's major abiotic sink\u2014retro-aldol cleavage to glycine and acetaldehyde\u2014is explicitly included. Feedstock and intermediate choices reflect only demonstrated or chemically justifiable steps, per the literature. Hydrothermal routes are excluded for final threonine accumulation due to demonstrated instability.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"HOCH2CN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydroxyacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H3NO/