lausible in warm, mineral-rich conditions (Patel et al., 2015; Danger et al., 2012)."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Aldol condensation: glycine + acetaldehyde (Akabori route)", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_017", "mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(II) ion"], "conditions": {"temperature": "310-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(II) ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Mild base"}, "mechanism": "Cu(II)-glycinate promotes carbanion formation and aldol condensation with acetaldehyde to threonine (\u00b1allo), accompanied by acetic acid and water.", "reasoning": "Akabori chemistry reported to yield threonine + allothreonine (Sato et al., 1957; Akabori, 1953; reviewed in Deep Research Findings; see also Wang et al., 2021 for extensive references)."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Glycine from HCN via Strecker synthesis", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": null}, "mechanism": "Formaldehyde reacts with ammonia to form Schiff base; cyanide attacks to yield aminoacetonitrile and water.", "reasoning": "Strecker synthesis of glycine is prebiotically established (Strecker, 1850; reported in Miller 1953, Deep Research Findings)."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_012", "mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["montmorillonite clay"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "montmorillonite clay", "aqueous": true, "other": "Wet/dry cycles speed hydrolysis"}, "mechanism": "Nitrile group hydrolyzed by water (via amide) with clay catalysis to glycine and ammonia.", "reasoning": "Literature supports hydrolysis of Strecker aminonitriles to amino acids on mineral surfaces (Danger et al., 2012; Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Retro-aldol cleavage: threonine to glycine + acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Mildly alkaline"}, "mechanism": "Threonine undergoes retro-aldol cleavage to glycine plus acetaldehyde.", "reasoning": "This degradation route is a known major sink for abiotic threonine; supported by kinetic studies in Deep Research Findings (Aubrey et al., 2009)."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Full Strecker route from lactaldehyde", "description": "HCN and NH3 to glycolaldehyde; homologation and photoreduction yield acetaldehyde, then lactonitrile, lactaldehyde; Strecker aminonitrile and full hydrolysis yield threonine (\u00b1allo).", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_016"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Akabori aldol condensation route", "description": "Glycine furnished by Strecker\u2013hydrolysis; Acetaldehyde by photoreduction; these condense under Cu(II) catalysis yielding threonine plus acetic acid and water.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_004", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014", "mol_011", "mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Alternative Strecker\u2014cyanohydrin cycling", "description": "HCN + glycolaldehyde in equilibrium with glycolonitrile; downstream homologation and hydrolysis follow Strecker route as in P1.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_004", "mol_005", "mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Direct aminoacetonitrile hydrolysis to glycine and aldol to threonine", "description": "HCN + formaldehyde produce aminoace