and regeneration loop. With rxn_004 unbalanced, the network's main bottleneck remains. The retro-aldol reaction is justified thermodynamically and by observation.\"]\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Threonine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This synthesis network follows the two experiment-supported prebiotic strategies for threonine formation: (1) Strecker-type reactions from lactaldehyde, itself supplied by photoreduction and cyanohydrin cycling from simpler aldehydes/nitriles, and (2) Akabori-type aldol reactions between glycine and acetaldehyde under surface/mineral/metal-catalyzed conditions. The network integrates literature-documented atomistic sequences, ensuring full atom balance in all reactions (including explicit tracking of H2O and NH3 byproducts). All reactions are strictly mass and charge balanced. Ten alternative routes reflect variations in key precursor formation and hydrolysis order, as well as in the aldol and Strecker branches. Retro-aldol cleavage of threonine is included as the major degradation sink. Diastereomeric pairs are tracked only at threonine formation steps, as per the research consensus that prebiotic syntheses are nonselective. Hydrothermal and protometabolic routes are excluded on literature evidence of their non-viability for threonine. All chemical and environmental assignments strictly follow both the experimental data and stoichiometric plausibility.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"a